ion","Congressional elections","Constitution and constitutional amendments","District of Columbia","Elections, voting, political campaign regulation","Government buildings, facilities, and property","House of Representatives","Legislative rules and procedure","Members of Congress","Presidents and presidential powers, Vice Presidents","State and local government operations"],"sponsorBioguideId":"N000147","cosponsorCount":204,"committeeNames":[],"congressDotGovUrl":"https://www.congress.gov/bill/119th-congress/house-bill/51","industries":[{"industry":"government","confidence":0.9,"rationale":"The bill fundamentally restructures the governance of the District, creating a new state government and redefining the federal district."},{"industry":"real-estate","confidence":0.7,"rationale":"Statehood would change property tax authority and land-use regulation for most of the current District, affecting developers and property owners."},{"industry":"legal","confidence":0.6,"rationale":"The transition of DC courts, judicial proceedings, and contractual obligations to state authority would affect legal practice and litigation in the new jurisdiction."}],"plainSummary":"This bill would admit most of the current District of Columbia as the 51st state, called Washington, Douglass Commonwealth, with two senators and one representative in Congress. A small federal district containing the Capitol, White House, Supreme Court, and nearby monuments would remain as the seat of the federal government. The bill sets up a transition process for DC laws, courts, contracts, and obligations to continue under state authority, and creates a commission to manage the shift to statehood.","classificationMethod":"llm","plain":{"whoItTouches":"Anyone who votes.","confidence":"crs-summary"},"topOverlap":{"bioguideId":"M001245","score":0.050448841212731466},"overlapCount":197},"meaning":{"percentile":41.67849818583373,"median":0.01073720309570498,"n":6339,"ordinary":[{"kind":"state-industry","text":"La